  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2606.17184},
  urldate = {2026-09-29},
  abstract = {This Letter reports on measurements of dijet transverse momentum (\$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T\textbraceright\$) imbalance and azimuthal acoplanarity in proton-proton collisions at \$\textbackslash sqrt\textbraceleft s\textbraceright{} = 200\$\textasciitilde GeV, using data recorded by the sPHENIX detector at the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider corresponding to an integrated luminosity of \$41\$\textasciitilde pb\$\textasciicircum\textbraceleft -1\textbraceright\$. Jets are reconstructed using the anti-\$k\_t\$ algorithm with radius parameters \$R = 0.3\$ to \$0.8\$ from electromagnetic and hadronic calorimeter energy deposits. The jet \$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T\textbraceright\$ resolution is determined directly in data using two independent methods. The dijet \$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T\textbraceright\$ imbalance is characterized by the ratio \$x\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft J\textbraceright{} = p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T,2\textbraceright/p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T,1\textbraceright\$ where \$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T,1(2)\textbraceright\$ is the highest (second-highest) jet \$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T\textbraceright\$ in the event. The dijet azimuthal acoplanarity \${$\Delta\varphi$}= \textbar{$\varphi\_$}1 - {$\varphi\_$}2\textbar\$ is also reported. Results are reported for different \$p\_\textbackslash mathrm\textbraceleft T,1\textbraceright\$ selections and jet radius parameters, normalized per dijet pair, and compared to the results of \textbackslash textsc\textbraceleft Pythia\textbraceright{} and \textbackslash textsc\textbraceleft Herwig\textbraceright{} Monte Carlo event generators. These measurements provide a stringent quantitative test of the modeling of QCD parton shower and hadronization dynamics, place important constraints on event-generator descriptions at RHIC energies, and establish a comprehensive proton-proton baseline for forthcoming measurements of jet modification in heavy ion collisions.},
